00:01:14.635 --> 00:01:17.590
and proofs of concepts
around the AI use cases

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.160
focused on those.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.580
We base our strategic
innovations on six services.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.570
I promise it's the last
slide about Atos itself.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.930
We help customers increase
their application agility.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:37.540
Our digital transformation
business unit does that.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.260
We also enable SAP on
Cloud for our customers.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.750
We drive more IoT
focused analysis

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.610
in terms of connecting the
cloud and IoT worlds together.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.400
We've done on-prem
services for the customer.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.500
Digital workplace
is the fourth point

00:01:53.500 --> 00:02:00.100
about collaboration in real-time
on Office 365, G Suite,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.840
and over cloud services.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.350
As I said, artificial
intelligence services

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.710
for customers leveraging
Google technologies,

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.760
and the one point where
I will focus today

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:19.240
is hybrid cloud for customers
in a private on-prem

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.330
and a public cloud environment.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.030
I will specifically
focus on the security

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:30.040
and start with a few concepts
that I believe should be core

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.590
and should be the
basis of understanding

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.500
between our customers,
ourselves, and the cloud

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.710
service providers.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.340
As you see sometimes,
there are now

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.560
some customers that are coming
back from the public cloud

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:53.110
to on-prem for their services,
because they misunderstood

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.550
the concept of shared
responsibility.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.290
I think a lot of
people talk about that,

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.260
so it's getting more and more
in the heads of everyone.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.390
I wanted to speak
about that again

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.000
today to make sure
that it's clear.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.180
When we move our services
to have infrastructure

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.700
as a services cloud
service provider,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.400
we still are
responsible from the way

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.840
we configure our virtual
network, our workloads,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.740
firewalling, the platform,
the applications, identity

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.390
and access management.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.170
And ultimately,
even in a SaaS mode,

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.070
we are still
responsible for data.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:41.740
Gartner predicts that
through 2023, more than 99%

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.230
of security failures in a
public cloud environment

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.180
will be due to a
misconfiguration

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.330
from the customer incorrectly
setting up access controls

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:53.320
on packets on a virtual
machine, incorrectly

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:58.260
configuring a web application
firewall, et cetera.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:03.780
That's where it's important to
focus our security technologies

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.580
and security service correctly
when we move to the cloud.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.000
When I say cloud, I encompass
IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.370
services, and
making sure that we

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.760
know where is our
most sensitive data

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.860
and where it's being moved.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.160
We need to identify it and
start if we are not doing it.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.100
If we did indeed
not do it yet, start

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.220
to know where is the
most sensitive data.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.010
Even if we don't go to a
full classification model,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.900
at least identify the
most sensitive data.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:42.560
In order to apply what I believe
is the core security domains

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.450
that we are to protect
the data, which

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.380
is managing the identities and
accesses of people and objects

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.880
that are accessing your data.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.950
When I say objects,
I speak about IoT,

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.890
but I speak also about
any kind of machine,

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:59.000
any kind of server
application, API,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.870
that is accessing your data or
requesting access to your data,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.820
and apply encryption to
the most sensitive data.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.710
There is no point-- and
I will show it later--

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.860
there is no point in
applying strong encryption

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.660
to all your data.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.420
So you should really focus
on the most sensitive items.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.040
Once you have done
that, you need also

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.260
to control your virtual network.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.030
And it will be more
and more difficult

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:30.740
as we see that the
boundaries are expanding,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.670
and that when we think about
the fact that we are [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.700
and our business units use
not only IaaS services,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:42.320
but also SaaS services, like
Salesforce, Workday, Dropbox,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.220
and G Suite, then the new
perimeter of the enterprise

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.830
is driven by the
data that is moving.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.500
I don't speak any
more about that.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.370
I speak more and more about
the perimeter of the data

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.560
where is the data moving,
and that's a big challenge

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:00.470
to understand.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.480
Once you've set up
your security controls,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.380
never assume that
you are 100% secure,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.360
that nothing will happen.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:14.150
You still have to really
focus on how I can detect,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.350
and in a timely manner respond,
appropriately to events.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.570
Always key and
understand also which

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:28.330
configurations I may have
missed or may have overlooked.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:33.645
Then, in order to make sure
that you know where the data is

00:06:33.645 --> 00:06:38.920
moving, focus on controlling
the use of SaaS services

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.170
and IaaS services by
your employee base

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.520
or by your business units.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.300
This is mostly helpful and done
by solutions like CASB, Cloud

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.040
Access Security Brokers.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:57.910
Gartner predicts that by
2022, 60% of large enterprises

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.870
will use a CASB, while today
I think it's a proximity 20%.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.000
Understanding the use of Shadow
IT and SaaS by your employees

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.830
will also allow you to better
target your security awareness

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.670
and identify what new
services are being adopted

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:20.270
by your employees, to
either secure the services

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:25.090
or propose to your employees
secure alternatives.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.700
Once we have said that, what
we mostly have forgotten

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.590
is that security, as
an IDC study has shown,

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:36.120
is not only the number one
concern for a public cloud

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.460
migration program.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:42.430
It's also paradoxically
the number three driver

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.110
for moving to the cloud.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:50.530
I see cloud technologies
as an opportunity

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:54.100
to do security better.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:57.520
We need to shift a
lot of our mindset

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.810
and to adopt these
new technologies to be

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:05.710
able to tackle some
concerns or some obstacles

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.160
that we are not able
to tackle on-prem.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.887
When I think about
public cloud, I

00:08:13.887 --> 00:08:15.470
don't think any about
virtual machines

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:18.490
that we set up on
a GCP environment,

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:22.570
but also about the
possibility to automate,

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:26.210
to use infrastructure as a code
for automatically deploying

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:34.419
our workloads to a CICD pipeline
or an immutable infrastructure.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:38.650
This is a way for us to automate
the delivery of security

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.990
settings, security agents.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.390
Embedding our security
agents and settings

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:48.970
into the templates of our IT
when they deploy new machines

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.940
is a way to make sure
that our controls are

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.280
deployed consistently.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:58.360
It helps also in terms
of security habits.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.880
Because if you are sure that all
your templates are configured,

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:07.210
you know all your
protection is deployed.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:15.250
Once we use these
strategies, and we

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:19.120
move to a more
auto-scalable infrastructure

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.990
and focus on total scalability
where on-demand, according

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:28.690
to the load of customers,
we provision new machines,

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.790
we will move to a world
where the traditional legacy

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:36.450
static firewalls splitting
our infrastructure

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:40.820
into three or four segments
will not be enough anymore.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.440
So controlling it still will
be critical and important

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.560
to control what we call the
north-south communication

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:51.220
from the internet to your
environment, from your on-prem

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:56.500
to your cloud environment, also
from more secured zones that

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.270
use internet.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:01.390
But you will have to ensure
also that you monitor

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.780
what is happening between your
machines, your workloads, which

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.790
can be virtual machines
or containers, as we

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:09.790
have micro-segmentation.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:13.930
So enforcing security
controls, firewalling,

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:17.290
intrusion prevention,
packet inspection,

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.990
entering and outgoing
of every machine.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:23.920
And by this leveraging the tags
and labels on your machines,

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.450
that are something that has been
already adopted by the DevOps

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:32.290
entities to make sure that
when they deploy new workloads

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.750
they know what these
new workloads are doing.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:38.290
This is an opportunity
for security professionals

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:42.670
to also use that in
order to better automate

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:45.820
the firewalling and the
delivery of security agents.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.585
One thing that we do is
pushing all our partners

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:55.550
and all our security
vendors to make sure

00:10:55.550 --> 00:11:00.740
that they adopt unified security
posture, that I will explain

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.520
later, which is one central
console to orchestrate

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:09.860
their solutions across on-prem
platforms and public cloud

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:11.490
platforms.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:18.020
With this central console of
the security vendors being

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:24.000
open to API calls, meaning
that these API calls need

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.820
to be secure, by
taking this assumption

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.940
we will be able to make
all these solutions

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.460
or these vendors
working together.

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:36.960
One of the many security issues
I see in security today, which

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.390
is the speed of closing the
doors when an attack occurs,

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:45.570
or the speed of the response
to an event, whether it is

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:50.070
a security event coming from
a log or a misconfiguration

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:51.670
that you have detected.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:56.250
So doing that and
enforcing that,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:12:02.520
we are able to really automate
and orchestrate our security.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:06.720
It's something that I
will dig in further later.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.130
Also, test your application.

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:17.940
Use application security testing
tools to help your DevOps

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.920
understand which
[INAUDIBLE] abilities

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.100
they have in the code,
the business code

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.392
that the business applications
that they are developing

00:12:24.392 --> 00:12:26.730
and open source
libraries they are using.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:31.040
The application security testing
vendors are adopting DevOps

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:35.720
are connecting or opening their
tools to a CICD tools, Jenkins,

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:41.250
GitHub, JIRA, to be able to
automatically scan new code

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.800
and scan code as it's edited.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.510
And most critical within
IT, create a ticket,

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:50.040
but fix ticket in JIRA,
so your developers see

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.050
that as a new bug fix for them.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:59.600
Once we have done all that,
the next step is, from my point

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:04.350
of view, know that your
production, when you have

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:08.550
a four-party solution or a
piece of code that is vulnerable

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:10.650
and you need to
update it, you don't

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:12.060
need to patch the production.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.580
What you need to do is
update your templates,

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:19.620
like I mentioned
earlier, test them,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.482
and then your production
of your environment

00:13:21.482 --> 00:13:23.190
will be honored with
these new templates.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:26.520
You need to ensure that
you kill your existing

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:31.020
workloads in a structured
manner and stuff the new ones

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:32.440
with the new template.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.500
So we don't have to
negotiate anymore,

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:38.970
which is what was one of the
painful parts negotiating

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:43.070
maintenance windows
with the business.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.060
They always say
that they are 24/7.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.580
They cannot afford
for a downtime.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.180
So now there is no more
downtime with this strategy.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:54.510
You update the templates,
you kill the machines,

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.200
and start new ones.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:59.280
And when you have done that,
you can push the logic forward

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:07.150
and see that you don't need
your machines, your workload,

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:11.350
as they are multiplying
horizontally to be one

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.450
single one to be up forever.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:18.340
You can set an expiration
time, let's say eight hours,

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.020
and say that all
your workloads will

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:26.980
expire after eight hours and
your auto-scalability engine

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:31.310
will monitor [INAUDIBLE]
managed instance groups,

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:32.560
will monitor all the workload.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:36.840
If this workload was necessary,
it will spin up a new one

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:39.700
starting from your template.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.230
You know that your
environment will

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:44.860
be renewed after
your [INAUDIBLE] will

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.420
be honored after eight hours.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:49.420
Of course, this is applicable
only to applications

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:52.200
that are compatible, that are
cloud- native applications,

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.310
are compatible
with this approach.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:55.900
But as we move
forward, that is what

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:58.510
I see-- moving from
[INAUDIBLE] applications

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:02.860
to these cloud
native applications.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.260
When you do that, you will
remove the persistence

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:09.400
of your environment and
make it harder for attackers

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:17.230
to discover your environment
When they start plugging

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:20.140
an agent or a Trojan
on your environment,

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:23.330
it will be removed after
eight hours Of course,

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:29.620
this is only valid if you make
sure that they cannot tamper

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:33.130
with your templates, and your
templates become your most

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:38.110
valuable assets,
along with your data.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:41.720
So once I did that, this
introduction was really

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:48.550
to focus on your data and
the code, so the templates

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:53.040
and the data that drives your
business and your applications.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.630
And once I said that the data
is the most important part,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:00.660
to enforce data
security-centric features,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.370
I will focus on two features
that I will point out

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:07.840
now-- identity and access
management, and encryption.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:11.020
So first, very
quickly, I believe

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:14.140
this is something that is
pretty clear for everyone.

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:16.060
But I will take
it again, which is

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:22.420
how does the Identity Provider,
the IdP, work with [INAUDIBLE]??

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:26.200
One customer, one user,
makes a request to the cloud

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:30.090
environment-- to Google
Cloud or to G Suite,

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:31.510
to GCP or to G Suite--

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:36.840
is presented with a G Suite
login page, login field.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:42.820
I enter as a log in,
Vasco.Gomes@Atos.net,

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:46.780
and it's redirected to the
identity provider configured

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:53.410
for the Atos.net domain, which
is a third-party provider where

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:57.010
we have federated
our identities with.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:59.350
Once this is passed to an
identity provider which

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:04.240
makes the request to
the user, presents

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:08.109
a new page to the user, and
the user enters his password,

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:11.560
or enters any other
identification method,

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.380
could be a strong
two-factor authentication.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.900
And once this is done,
the identity provider

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.970
validates the identity of the
user and his access context,

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.550
and delivers a token
both to the user

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:28.569
and the secret to the
cloud service provider.

00:17:28.569 --> 00:17:32.560
And the user is then entitled to
present this token to the cloud

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:37.050
service provider,
which can then allow

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.740
him access to his environment.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:44.180
So once we've said
that, and we are--

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:46.790
Atos is a global managed
security service company

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:49.070
delivering services
to another cloud

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:53.570
to more than 200 customers,
large enterprises.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.400
We are facing with
a difficulty, which

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:59.480
is how do we manage all these
complexities of every customer

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.180
having his own identity
provider and accessing

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.820
a multiple set of solutions?

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:08.850
It could be a
software-as-a-service solution,

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:13.360
like ServiceNow,
TIBCO, Office 365.

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:19.340
It could be an API
gateway, like Apigee.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:23.300
This is a complexity to
manage and to set up.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.060
You have to set up the
environment everywhere.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:31.760
And also some providers are
not multiple IdP compatible,

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:32.690
like TIBCO Spotfire.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.240
What we've done is
transformed our solutions

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:41.930
to work as an IdP hotel.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:47.780
So the customer can still
rely on his own IdP provider.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.000
There are a lot of
them now on the market.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.388
And we don't touch
anything on these.

00:18:53.388 --> 00:18:54.930
We just hold for a
while the request.

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:01.730
The feature is called Where
Are You From feature in SAML.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:06.890
So we do-- when the
365 G Suite provides us

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:13.700
with the authentication, we set
up our Atos Evidian web access

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:19.260
manager as IdP for
the service provider.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:22.860
And in the customer
environment, we

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:27.070
set up Atos Evidian as a
cloud service provider.

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:29.910
So we insert ourselves
into the chain,

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.430
and we enable by
that to be simplified

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:37.500
in terms of management,
in terms of monitoring,

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.100
and also for TIBCO
Spotfire-- for example,

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:45.950
it sees only one IdP,
which is our IdP.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.860
Once we have done that, we can
plug our identity governance

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:53.330
and administration solution.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:20:01.340
It's a workflow management
certification solution,

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.940
which will sell for the customer
information security office

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:10.440
team as a single location to
define all their profiles.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.130
So we are bringing
different profiles,

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:16.940
and they can define for
every role and profile who

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:20.180
from their teams are
integral to this profile.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:25.640
It could be a Linux
administrator,

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.250
SAP administrator.

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:34.280
It could be a folder organizer
or an executive assistant

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:36.500
in there for
collaborative services.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.730
And this is defined in
our central location.

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:42.860
And we are adapting
our profile templates

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:47.540
to be multi-cloud available.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.720
So once a customer
wants to move workloads

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:54.500
from one cloud
provider to another,

00:20:54.500 --> 00:21:00.480
do a migration from
a competitor to GCP,

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:04.400
it doesn't change the templates
that we use in our solution.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.700
We just un-provision
automatically

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:11.450
the access rights and
the groups and users

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.775
from the previous environment.

00:21:13.775 --> 00:21:17.720
And we do the API calls to
Google Cloud Identity, which

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:22.430
is the IAM model of
a Google, to newly

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:26.760
provision the users, the groups,
and access rights in GCP.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:31.610
So it eases the migration from
a cloud service provider to GCP.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:39.250
We provide a single
point of administration.

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:44.120
[INAUDIBLE] provides
a single location

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.400
where they can see all
of their access rights,

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:51.440
and they will come back on
all of their cloud service

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.730
providers.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:56.900
When you do an audit,
you don't have the time

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:00.400
to go to every one of your
cloud service providers--

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:05.690
should it be GCP, G Suite,
Dropbox, Salesforce, Workday.

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:10.700
You simply go to the
Atos Evidian interface,

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:16.220
and you see who is
entitled to which role

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:17.660
of all your
[INAUDIBLE] providers.

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:22.580
And you have the traceability
of the activities, which

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:28.910
also gives you a single button
where when an employee leaves

00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:32.840
the company, you just
go to the console,

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:35.300
remove his access rights.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:38.190
Or if you have
integrated your IdP

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:41.000
with ours, which
is the objective,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:44.750
we detect with our IdP that
the user has disappeared

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:46.830
from your directory.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:54.290
The IdP part of our solution
communicates with the identity

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:58.370
governance administration to
un-provision the customer.

00:22:58.370 --> 00:23:03.320
So for example, in
GCP, when you provision

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:06.940
an account in G Suite,
and a user authenticates,

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:10.380
each session is active
for two weeks by default.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:16.800
So when a user is
removed, he leaves

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.610
the company, even if you
remove it from your directory,

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:26.540
the IdP session when
you authenticate,

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:28.910
lasts for 14 days.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:34.500
So if when you
remove his account,

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:40.550
he has open a session from
his own unmanaged device

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:43.850
or his own laptop at
home, this session

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:47.930
can be still open until 14 days.

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:53.690
One way of enforcing and
removing these sessions

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:59.270
would be to go to the GCP
console or the G Suite console,

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:03.940
and you do a reset which
kills all these sessions.

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:06.440
What you have to do is a purge.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:13.220
What we are doing here in
the solution is automating.

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:15.763
As the IdP detects that
the user has been removed

00:24:15.763 --> 00:24:17.180
from the directory,
we communicate

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:20.570
with the identity and access
management, our identity

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:22.490
governance and
administration solution,

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:28.010
and we do the API call to G
Suite to reset the password

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:30.172
and then kill all the sessions.

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:36.880
The possibilities and the
use cases are endless.

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:42.040
The strings have coupled
web access management, IdP,

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:47.120
and identity governance
and administration.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:49.880
We provide the single
point of administration.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:56.420
We avoid when the customer
spins up a new cloud service

00:24:56.420 --> 00:24:58.670
provider, he doesn't have
to hook up all his IdPs.

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:04.130
We just do it from the
cloud IAM solution.

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.670
And by doing that, we
avoid also the complexity

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:11.840
in a cloud migration of adapting
all your access rights from one

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:13.460
cloud provider to another.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:19.310
It's very-- the identity and
access management model of GCP

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.450
is very different
from its competitors.

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:26.360
So it could be a key blocker for
moving from a competitor to--

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:28.880
your workloads from
a competitor to GCP.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:32.060
Because you have
to not only deal

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:34.670
with the complexity of
moving your workloads,

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:37.610
but also the complexity
of adapting your IAM.

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:39.740
With this strategy,
you are completely

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:44.300
free from this issue.

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:47.930
I also mentioned earlier
about managing your identities

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:50.420
and access to your data is key.

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:55.750
And in some cases, you can also,
for your most sensitive data,

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:56.750
want to encrypt them.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:04.860
In GCP, by default, all
your data is encrypted.

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.810
So GCP does default encryption.

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:09.430
But this default
encryption, that you

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:13.400
see in the left
of the picture, is

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:18.590
done with their default
key management server.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.360
You have no control, no access.

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:23.570
You cannot revoke
the encryption keys.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:28.070
You cannot spin up different
encryption keys for different

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:30.710
environments.

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:33.830
For that Google has a service
called Customer Managed

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:36.410
Encryption Keys, CMEK.

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:38.750
It's basically exactly
the same service.

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:42.320
You have the same key
management server from Google,

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.450
but this time you
have an interface,

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:48.230
and you have access to the
Google KMS, Key Management

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:49.010
Server.

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:51.200
And you can affect
different keys

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:54.800
to different business
units or different business

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:55.730
applications.

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:57.590
You can revoke keys.

00:26:57.590 --> 00:26:58.820
You can rotate keys.

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.200
If you have a doubt about
the security leakage,

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:10.233
you can go to the key management
server and revoke the keys.

00:27:10.233 --> 00:27:11.150
You have more control.

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:14.090
But still, for some
customers, they

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:19.630
don't want to put all the
eggs in the same basket.

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:22.400
For the most
sensitive data, they

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:26.720
would want to have more
control on the way the keys are

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:30.900
generated and the ways
the keys are stored.

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:33.000
So for that, there
is another option

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:38.900
in Google, which is called
Google Customer Supplied

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:40.230
Encryption Keys.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:44.060
It's an API of tools
of Google communicating

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:48.560
with your key management server
that you can host on-prem

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:52.640
or on a private cloud.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:58.360
It enables you to
use your own solution

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.340
to generate your own
key management server

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:03.910
to create keys and store them.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:07.840
When a Google Cloud
Service needs a key,

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.690
you have to provide it
to CSEK so we can decrypt

00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:13.990
the workload or the data.

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:18.970
When I spoke earlier
about the need

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:22.620
to classify and to
understand your data,

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:25.710
you could not use
all the solutions

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:33.600
that Google gives to you if you
don't know where your data is.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:38.310
There is no point in encrypting
with Customer Supplied

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:43.770
Encryption Key all your
data, because as we know,

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:46.380
the more control you have
on your keys, the less

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.780
compatibility you have
with Google GCP services.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:51.660
So the default
encryption, of course,

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:54.900
is compatible with
all GCP products.

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.930
The customer managed encryption
keys, the version where

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:00.690
you have access to the key
management server of Google,

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:04.410
is only compatible with GCP
application layer encryption

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:07.290
and GPC products--

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:11.020
Google Cloud Storage, the
packets, Google Compute Engine,

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:14.250
the virtual machines,
BigQuery and Dataproc.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:17.500
And as we speak, Google is
enhancing their compatibility,

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:20.820
but still is not
all the products.

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:25.500
When you go to bring your own
key strategy, CSEK, Customer

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.120
Supplied Encryption
Keys, it's only

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:30.835
compatible with Google Cloud
Storage and Google Compute

00:29:30.835 --> 00:29:31.350
Engine.

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:39.875
It's a balance between
the trust and agility.

00:29:42.715 --> 00:29:44.840
The more trust you have in
the control of your data

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:47.370
and the encryption keys,
the less compatibility

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:49.230
you have with Google services.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:52.412
But you might, for your
most sensitive data,

00:29:52.412 --> 00:29:54.120
for the most critical
data, you might not

00:29:54.120 --> 00:30:01.410
need to use all the
Google services but only

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.480
specific ones.

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:06.330
That's one thing
we help you to do

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:12.870
is to make sure that
you can set up the best

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:16.440
approach between these
four registry layers

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:20.690
according to the
sensitivity of the data.

00:30:27.695 --> 00:30:29.070
One thing that
I've not mentioned

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:31.320
is the encryption tool used.

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.880
In all these cases, I have
spoken about the default

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:41.620
encryption and the customer
supplied encryption keys

00:30:41.620 --> 00:30:45.300
when you use your key
management's server on your

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:46.290
[INAUDIBLE].

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:49.245
There was a line on top
that is [INAUDIBLE] module,

00:30:49.245 --> 00:30:49.995
which is optional.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:57.570
For greater trust
and greater entropy

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:01.230
and the greater randomization
of the keys generated,

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.690
you could generate
them with hardware.

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:05.760
When you generate
a key software,

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:10.800
use a random function of
the operating system, which

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:13.440
is less reliable in
terms of the strength

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:19.090
of the key than hardware
based, but it's optional.

00:31:19.090 --> 00:31:25.490
And all that is always using
the cloud provider encryption

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:28.550
tools, so in the case where
you bring your own key

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:31.640
you use Costumer Supplied
Encryption Keys solution

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:34.940
from Google, which is
still a Google solution.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:38.600
So they still need you to
bring your key at the moment

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:41.090
of the decryption of data.

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.160
They don't have access
to all your encryption

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:45.650
keys at all times,
but at the moment

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:50.520
the authorized user
needs to access the data,

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:53.200
the key needs to be provided
to the cloud providers who

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:59.670
decrypts the data and provides
it unencrypted to the user.

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:02.380
So the first step
is, how can we build,

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:06.350
have more control in the
encryption tool itself,

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:14.270
and replace CSEK or CMEK
for encryption of Google,

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:19.160
but rather use your own
key management server

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:23.640
and fob and your own
encryption solution

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:28.860
and not provide the key to CSEK?

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:34.620
Our solution, which is called
Atos TrustWay Data Product,

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:37.200
is a global key
management server

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:40.680
embedded in a hardware security
module that you can host

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:43.520
on-prem or in a private cloud.

00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:45.780
In Atos, we can
deliver it as a service

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:50.400
also where the keys are
generated here and managed

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:54.330
and stored in these [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:58.440
We provide connectors
and integration

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:02.550
to encrypt file systems on
Linux and Windows, et cetera.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:05.570
We do database
encryption Oracle, DB2,

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:10.650
to MSQL Virtual Machine
encryption, application

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.690
encryption, and
tokenization, which

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:17.290
is only replacing
data with a token

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:20.400
and keeping an index
of these tokens.

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:24.750
We also have connectors for
MongoDB, [? Hadoop ?] HBase,

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:26.790
and multiple other services.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:33.660
Deploying such services on
GCP and using this service,

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:37.110
you can afford to provide
the keys to a provider,

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:41.875
like your legacy,
like Google, and have

00:33:41.875 --> 00:33:44.890
a single pane of glass, a single
controller, a single console,

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:50.380
to control all your encryption
on-prem under IaaS or PaaS

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:51.140
services.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:54.400
In SaaS services and
[INAUDIBLE]---- so integrate,

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:58.120
we have connectors with the
main CASB providers-- netskope,

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:01.090
McAfee MVISION,
[INAUDIBLE],, Blue Coat,

